<h2 id="verdict"><span class="sec-no">1</span>Verdict</h2>
<div class="verdict">
  <p><strong>Roughly 85–90% of the challenge's surface area is built, and built with unusually good intent</strong> — a two-part router/evaluator design with a nameable routing signal, cache-aware pricing with correct reset logic, an interactive lab, a live presentation deck, and honesty notes engineered into the artifacts themselves. Every headline number reproduces exactly from committed artifacts; nothing is fabricated.</p>
  <p><strong>But three things must be fixed before this is presentable.</strong> First, a license breach: the dataset is publicly redistributed via the git history of the public GitHub repo. Second, the cross-validation that underwrites every out-of-fold number is broken — 76% of trajectories fell into one accidental group, so the claimed tenant-safe folds mostly never ran. Third, the headline "61.4% exact, nested-validated" depends on a dispatch rule calibrated on test-set label marginals; the deployable analogue on the same folds scores ~49%. A fourth, the λ-Bayes dispatch, routes using each task's hindsight cost — its numbers are an oracle upper bound, not a router result.</p>
  <p>The good news: the fixes are mostly small code changes plus a rerun, the honest reframings mostly <em>strengthen</em> the story (one "not significant" claim is actually significant in the team's favor), and the judging criteria explicitly reward named weaknesses.</p>
</div>
