<h2 id="good"><span class="sec-no">3</span>What is genuinely good</h2>
<p class="sec-sub">Verified, not taken on faith — two reviewers independently recomputed the published numbers from the committed artifacts.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>The core hygiene rules hold.</strong> The router reads only routing-time sources (system prompt, first user message, tool definitions); the logged model id never enters features or labels; TF-IDF, SVD, and classifiers are fit per training fold everywhere; the evaluator is frozen during router tuning. This was checked line by line.</li>
  <li><strong>Every quoted number is real arithmetic on real artifacts.</strong> The 61.4/54.2/95.6 headline, the full round-1 frontier table, the τ/λ operating points, the logged-dispatch point, the $147.71 penalty, and every row of <code>model_tiers.json</code> and <code>matching_check.json</code> were reproduced to the last digit. The problems are methodological, not fabrication.</li>
  <li><strong>Cache-reset pricing is correct.</strong> Call 0 pays uncached, the shared item-prefix is billed cached only when the model is unchanged, the first call after a switch pays the full prefix — verified with no off-by-one in both <code>scripts/cost_model.py</code> and <code>cache_trap.py</code>.</li>
  <li><strong>The ordinal head is a correct Frank–Hall construction</strong> (two cumulative logits, monotonicity clamp, valid class probabilities), and the nested-stacking implementation in <code>experiments.py</code> is correct.</li>
  <li><strong>Honest negatives are reported on purpose:</strong> tuned weights losing to defaults, clustering dominated, the "INCONCLUSIVE" matched check, the null result on revealed preference, and a 30-perturbation evaluator sensitivity pass. This is above hackathon standard.</li>
  <li><strong>The dashboard is faithful and fast.</strong> A Node replication of the JS math reproduced the Python results within tie-handling noise; full recompute measured ~1 ms (k-means ~29 ms); k-means is seeded and deterministic; there are no event-listener leaks; all defaults match the documentation; <code>findings.js</code> mirrors the result JSONs value-for-value.</li>
  <li><strong>The two-part design is the right idea:</strong> router and evaluator read disjoint slices of each trajectory, and the presentation structure (one chart, one claim, one named weakness) matches what judges score.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="critical"><span class="sec-no">4</span>Critical findings</h2>
<p class="sec-sub">Each of these invalidates a headline claim or breaks a hard rule. All are fixable this weekend.</p>

<div class="finding crit">
  <h4><span class="fid">C1</span><span class="pill crit">License breach</span> The dataset is publicly redistributed through git history</h4>
  <p>The GitHub repo <code>sshibinthomass/entire-viktor-tumai-ehk</code> is <strong>public</strong>, and the pushed history of <code>origin/master</code> still contains the full dataset: <code>trajectories_v1_01.jsonl.tar.gz</code> as a raw 31.7 MB blob, plus a Git-LFS pointer to the 105 MB JSONL (commit <code>c21aece</code>; the later "remove from tracking" commit <code>261c20c</code> removed the files from the working tree but not from history). Separately, <code>dashboard/data.js</code> and <code>results/router_features.jsonl</code> are committed and contain 140-character verbatim previews of every trajectory's first user message plus the logged model ids. AGENTS.md's hard rule: "never upload it anywhere or commit it to git."</p>
  <p class="fix">Make the repo private immediately. Purge the blobs with <code>git filter-repo</code> (or BFG), force-push all branches, and ask GitHub support to drop cached forks/views. Untrack or strip the <code>_preview</code>/<code>_trigger</code> fields from committed artifacts before re-publishing anything.</p>
</div>

<div class="finding crit">
  <h4><span class="fid">C2</span><span class="pill crit">Leakage / bug</span> The GroupKFold workspace fingerprint is broken for 76% of the data</h4>
  <p><code>workspace_of</code> (<code>router/ml.py:50</code>, duplicated in <code>tune_router.py:63</code>) reads the raw system content; when the content is a parts list it gets <code>json.dumps</code>'d, the newlines are escaped, the multiline regex matches nothing, and the fingerprint becomes <code>md5("")</code>. Verified in <code>results/diag_arrays.npz</code>: <strong>721 of 953 trajectories share the empty-string group</strong>. The actual folds are one 232-train/721-test split plus four 895/58 splits — not the claimed tenant-safe 5-fold CV. Any tenant whose system content appears in both formats straddles train and test, and 76% of all pooled OOF predictions come from one model trained on 232 rows with a very different label mix.</p>
  <p class="fix">Reuse the feature extractor's content-joining logic (<code>router/features.py:44</code>) inside <code>workspace_of</code>, then rerun every OOF number — the 61.4% headline, all frontier AUCs, and the tuning conclusions all sit on these folds. Report the group-size distribution alongside the result.</p>
</div>

<div class="finding crit">
  <h4><span class="fid">C3</span><span class="pill crit">Leakage</span> The 61.4% headline uses test-set label marginals to place its dispatch cuts</h4>
  <p><code>cut_dispatch</code> (<code>experiments.py:81</code>) sets tier thresholds from the <em>full</em> label vector's marginals and the <em>pooled</em> OOF score quantiles, and <code>exp_final.py:88</code> applies it globally — so every test task's tier depends on the true label mix of the test set. The inner CV does this correctly on train folds only; the outer, headline-producing step does not. Recomputed on the committed <code>final_probs.npz</code>: pooled dispatch 62.0% exact, <strong>train-fold-calibrated dispatch 49.2%</strong> on the same folds. The gap is inflated by C2's degenerate fold, but as shipped the number sold as "zero grid optimism" is load-bearing on test-label information.</p>
  <p class="fix">One-line change: calibrate cuts from train-fold labels and scores inside each outer fold (the inner loop at <code>exp_final.py:80</code> already shows how). Quote both numbers — "agreement given known deployment marginals" and the deployable figure — and make the deployable one the headline.</p>
</div>

<div class="finding crit">
  <h4><span class="fid">C4</span><span class="pill crit">Leakage</span> λ-Bayes dispatch routes on each task's realized, hindsight cost</h4>
  <p>The cost matrix inside <code>argmin(C + λ·P(D&gt;t))</code> is built from the evaluator's deepest-call metrics — <code>context_tokens</code>, <code>gen_tokens</code>, <code>n_llm_calls</code> (<code>tune_router.py:181–201</code>, <code>run_pipeline.py:162</code>) — i.e. the realized size of the trajectory, unknowable at dispatch. That is exactly why λ-Bayes "starves token-heavy tasks": it knows which tasks are token-heavy. The bolded best AUC in the round-1 table (0.903) and the "max tasks-per-dollar: 78.2% saved" operating point are oracle-cost upper bounds, not router results. Using realized cost as an <em>evaluation weight</em> is fine; using it <em>inside the dispatch</em> is not. The blend, heuristic-cuts, and τ methods are clean.</p>
  <p class="fix">Replace realized cost with a routing-time cost estimate (predict per-tier cost from routing features), re-sweep — or relabel every λ row and operating point as an oracle upper bound in SOLUTION.md, the dashboard, and the deck.</p>
</div>

<div class="finding crit">
  <h4><span class="fid">C5</span><span class="pill crit">Bug, upstream of everything</span> Trajectory grouping ignores the system prompt</h4>
  <p><code>group_key</code> (<code>scripts/load_trajectories.py:39</code>) hashes only <code>first_user_text[:2000]</code> — its own docstring and AGENTS.md both specify "system + first user text". Distinct tasks that open with the same generic user message (or an empty / image-only first message, which all hash to one key) merge into a single trajectory, corrupting router features (wrong "first" call), evaluator labels (inflated depth), and cache-aware costs. The symptom is visible in the shipped data: <code>results/routes.jsonl</code> contains 29 switches and mixed-model routes under a whole-trajectory policy that should produce zero for correctly grouped trajectories. Every downstream artifact inherits this key via <code>trajectory_id</code>.</p>
  <p class="fix">Hash system-prompt text + full first-user text (drop the 2000-char truncation), route empty keys to per-request singleton trajectories, then add a nesting validator: after sorting each group, assert call i's input items are a prefix of call i+1's, and split groups that fail. Regenerate everything and report how many trajectories changed — the validator itself is strong "evaluation rigor" material.</p>
</div>

<h2 id="major"><span class="sec-no">5</span>Major findings</h2>

<div class="finding major">
  <h4><span class="fid">M1</span><span class="pill major">Circularity</span> Tier inference and the matched check validate each other with the same signals</h4>
  <p><code>infer_tiers.py</code> assigns model tiers from within-bucket z-scores of tool-error rate and retry streak; <code>matching_check.py</code> then "validates" those tiers by comparing… tool-error rate and retry streak across them. Models were labeled low-tier partly <em>because</em> they struggled on hard buckets — selection on the outcome, not an independent natural experiment. Neither docstring names this.</p>
  <p class="fix">Split the signals: infer tiers from one outcome (or one half of trajectories) and validate on the other. At minimum, state the dependence in both scripts and SOLUTION.md.</p>
</div>

<div class="finding major">
  <h4><span class="fid">M2</span><span class="pill major">Circularity</span> The "strict domination over logged dispatch" claim is biased in the router's favor</h4>
  <p>The logged-dispatch point (89.9% served / 69.2% cost) prices each logged model at an inferred tier derived from error/streak z-scores — but those same signals also raise the evaluator's difficulty D (weight 0.10 for errors plus the ≥5-errors→D3 override). A model that logged errors is simultaneously assigned a low tier <em>and</em> its trajectories a high difficulty, mechanically depressing logged served%. The router's own tiers carry no such penalty. The tercile forcing (three ids per tier regardless of spread, with boundary bootstrap stabilities of 0.595 and 0.505) compounds it.</p>
  <p class="fix">For this comparison, grade difficulty without the error/streak metrics, report sensitivity to the tercile assignment, and phrase it as "under our inferred tier map" rather than strict domination.</p>
</div>

<div class="finding major">
  <h4><span class="fid">M3</span><span class="pill major">Wrong claim — in your favor</span> The matched check's "not significant" is false for its stronger effect</h4>
  <p><code>matching_check.py</code> bootstraps a CI only for the error-rate difference-in-differences; the retry-streak interaction (+1.33) is never tested, yet the <code>"significant": false</code> verdict and SOLUTION.md's "not significant at n=953" speak for both. Re-running the script's own bootstrap on the streak interaction gives a 95% CI of <strong>[+0.08, +2.63] — excludes zero</strong>. The honest headline flips to "streak effect significant (borderline), error effect not." Also: the DiD is computed from rounded table cells, and the error-rate signal is nearly flat, so the streak carries essentially all the evidence.</p>
  <p class="fix">Bootstrap CIs for both metrics with ≥5,000 resamples (the CI is borderline), compute from unrounded arrays, base the verdict on both, and update SOLUTION.md and the deck.</p>
</div>

<div class="finding major">
  <h4><span class="fid">M4</span><span class="pill major">Overstated</span> The cache-trap "demonstration" demonstrates nothing in-data</h4>
  <p>On the 24 measurable multi-call trajectories the cheap-tool-loops policy produces <strong>zero switches</strong> and identical naive/aware costs (overstatement: 0.0 points). The $147.71 penalty is a model extrapolation over all 953 trajectories under three stacked assumptions (prefix ≈ T/2 per switch, 2 switches per user turn, averaged rate delta). The JSON carries the caveat; SOLUTION.md reads as if measured. The reader also never gets the number they need: the policy's <em>corrected</em> savings on the same base (roughly 25–40%, not 96%).</p>
  <p class="fix">Label the $148 as modeled with its assumptions, compute and quote the corrected savings percentage, and check the <code>input[-2]</code> trigger-item assumption in <code>alternating_route</code> against a real export line — the Responses format suggests it should be <code>input[-1]</code>.</p>
</div>

<div class="finding major">
  <h4><span class="fid">M5</span><span class="pill major">Mislabeled denominator</span> "$259 all-Tier-3 input budget" includes $49 of output cost</h4>
  <p>The $259.18 decomposes as $109.76 first-pass input + $100.63 cached replay + $48.80 output; input-only is $210.39. "$148 is over half the input budget" survives against the correct base (70%) but the label is wrong in SOLUTION.md and the script output. Related: Tier 3 is priced at opus-5's row ($5/$25) while the inferred tier map puts fable-5 ($10/$50, the most expensive id) in Tier 3 — the all-T3 budget, the 48.6% savings, and the $148 framing all lean on the cheaper representative, and the sensitivity is never shown.</p>
  <p class="fix">Quote $210 input-only, and add a one-line tier-price sensitivity (opus-priced vs fable-priced T3) for the headline savings.</p>
</div>

<div class="finding major">
  <h4><span class="fid">M6</span><span class="pill major">Design gap</span> The shipped pipeline is transductive end-to-end with no inference path</h4>
  <p>Percentile ranks, blend ranks, and tier cuts are all computed on the batch being routed (<code>run_pipeline.py:146</code>, <code>ml.py:113</code>, <code>tiering.py:61</code>); nothing — ECDFs, TF-IDF vocabulary, LR coefficients, cut values — is ever serialized. A task's tier depends on which other tasks share the batch, the router cannot route one new task, and the shipped behavior differs from the per-fold protocol its own benchmarks enforce. This is also why no held-out-split run exists.</p>
  <p class="fix">Add a fit/freeze/apply path: serialize the fitted transforms and fixed cuts from chunk 01, and add a runner that applies them frozen to a new chunk. This closes the deck's "final run on held-out split" requirement at the same time.</p>
</div>

<div class="finding major">
  <h4><span class="fid">M7</span><span class="pill major">Consistency</span> The shipped heuristic is not the one that was benchmarked</h4>
  <p>Two rank transforms coexist: <code>percentile_ranks</code> (average-tie, used by the production pipeline) and <code>fold_ranks</code> (right-ECDF, used by every benchmark). For imbalanced binary features the 0-vs-1 contrast differs by ~5× between the two spaces, so the tuned defaults (α, group weights, cuts) were validated on a materially different composite than the one shipped. The dashboard adds a third variant, which is why the lab shows 61.3% where the writeup says 61.4% (2 of 953 tasks flip tier).</p>
  <p class="fix">Standardize on one transform (the benchmarked <code>fold_ranks</code>), port the same semantics to the JS, and re-quote the tuned defaults.</p>
</div>

<div class="finding major">
  <h4><span class="fid">M8</span><span class="pill major">Reproducibility</span> A clean checkout cannot reproduce anything</h4>
  <p><code>pyproject.toml</code> declares <code>dependencies = []</code> while the code needs numpy, scipy, and scikit-learn; <code>run_pipeline.py</code> defaults to a hardcoded <code>D:\Github-Projects\…</code> path and KeyErrors on a raw (un-enriched) export with no hint that <code>enrich_dataset.py</code> must run first; <code>results/exp_cache.npz</code> is absent so the experiment scripts can't re-run; the blend-frontier row and the α/τ/λ sweeps that produced the shipped defaults exist in no committed script; and <code>number_dataset.py</code> rewrites the raw export in place (truncation risk on crash, silently drops unknown fields, and breaks the documented 3-field schema). The submission skill requires exactly this reproduction to work.</p>
  <p class="fix">Declare dependencies, make the export path a required argument, commit the sweep scripts, regenerate <code>exp_cache.npz</code>, and make <code>number_dataset.py</code> write-to-temp-then-rename into a copy rather than mutating the raw export.</p>
</div>

<div class="finding major">
  <h4><span class="fid">M9</span><span class="pill major">Noisy signal → hard override</span> Tool-error detection is substring matching feeding a Difficulty-3 override</h4>
  <p><code>ERROR_MARKERS</code> checks the first 400 characters of tool output for substrings like "error" and "failed" (<code>evaluator/metrics.py:12,70</code>). "12 passed, 0 failed" counts as an error; a traceback after character 400 doesn't. This signal carries weight 0.10 in the difficulty score, drives the ≥5-errors→D3 hard override, and is one of the two outcomes behind the tier inference and matched check — so its noise propagates into three headline artifacts.</p>
  <p class="fix">Word-boundary regexes, scan the output tail (tracebacks end outputs), exclude negated patterns, and report marker precision on ~30 hand-checked outputs.</p>
</div>

<div class="finding major">
  <h4><span class="fid">M10</span><span class="pill major">Overstated</span> The "nested selection, zero grid optimism" claim doesn't hold up</h4>
  <p>The 8-config inner grid was pre-filtered by <code>exp_text.py</code>'s full sweep on the <em>same</em> GroupKFold folds, so the inner CV only re-picks within a shortlist already chosen by looking at OOF results; "selection bias ≈ 1 pt" is a lower bound, not zero. SOLUTION.md's "all five folds picked the same config independently" is false (<code>fold_picks</code>: 3× multinomial, 2× ordinal), and the nested result is bit-identical to a grid-selected row from <code>exp_text_report.json</code> — the nested run changed the tier of not one task.</p>
  <p class="fix">Re-run nested selection with fresh folds and the full grid after fixing C2, and correct the two SOLUTION.md sentences either way.</p>
</div>

<div class="finding major">
  <h4><span class="fid">M11</span><span class="pill major">Disclosed but unmitigated</span> Difficulty labels are policy-dependent and the correction is never applied</h4>
  <p>Effort metrics depend on which model served the trajectory (weaker models retry more). SOLUTION.md discloses this, and <code>matching_check.py</code> even measures it — but the evaluator never normalizes per model, so tasks historically served by weak models are labeled harder, and the ML router trains on exactly those labels, learning to over-route those workspaces.</p>
  <p class="fix">Residualize <code>n_tool_errors</code> and <code>max_repeat_streak</code> per inferred tier (subtract the per-tier median rank) before percentile ranking; the correction data already exists in the repo.</p>
</div>

<div class="finding major">
  <h4><span class="fid">M12</span><span class="pill major">Presentation</span> The deck fails its own quality gate in three small ways</h4>
  <p><code>present.html</code> still shows <code>[Team Name]</code> and <code>[Members — fill in]</code>; slide 5 hardcodes "~93% evaluator-config agreement" and "selection bias ≈ 1 pt" — neither exists in any artifact, against slide 1's "every number computed live" claim; and the frontier replay bounces τ endlessly without ever pinning the τ=0.80 operating point that slides 1 and 6 quote (92% @ 63%). Also, the quoted "93.4% / 80.4%" τ-frontier point matches no shipped grid (exact enumeration gives 93.8/81.6). The make-presentation skill additionally asks for the signal shown on a real trace snippet; slide 3 shows an aggregate chart only.</p>
  <p class="fix">Fill the placeholders, emit the two slide-5 numbers into a results JSON via <code>build_findings.py</code>, pause the replay at τ=0.80 with a marker ring, requote the frontier point from the shipped grid, and add one short real-trace snippet to slide 3.</p>
</div>

<h2 id="minor"><span class="sec-no">6</span>Minor findings</h2>
<p class="sec-sub">Real but small; batch-fix opportunistically.</p>
<div class="tbl-scroll"><table>
  <tr><th>Where</th><th>Finding</th></tr>
  <tr><td><code>load_trajectories.py:24</code></td><td>Files opened without <code>encoding="utf-8"</code> — on Windows (this team's platform) the cp1252 default can crash or misdecode the export; <code>enrich</code>/<code>number_dataset</code> inherit it. One-token fix.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>index.html:531</code></td><td>Tooltip double-decode: escaped dataset text is re-parsed as HTML, so redaction placeholders like <code>&lt;E&gt;</code> silently vanish on hover — and dataset-controlled markup would execute (injection path). Single-encode into text nodes.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>features.py</code> regexes</td><td>Noisy features: attachment regex matches "(and/or)"; trigger-header regex is unanchored; <code>tool_msteams</code> fires on any <code>_channel</code> tool; file-extension counter matches decimals like "3.14".</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>tiering.py:36</code> / <code>difficulty.py:44</code></td><td>Constant metrics rank to 0 instead of a neutral 0.5, deflating scores if a metric degenerates (matters when dashboard users re-weight).</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>ml.py:75,92</code></td><td>No class guard in the ordinal heads (a fold without D3 examples crashes) and <code>GroupKFold(5)</code> crashes on &lt;5 workspace groups.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>metrics.py:63</code></td><td><code>max_repeat_streak</code> never resets on user turns or assistant messages — a Bash-only session reads as one giant retry streak (observed: streak 17 spanning 11 assistant messages).</td></tr>
